.TH CCOL_SELECTABLE_FROM_CHAN 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_selectable_from_chan \- resolve a channel into a ccol_selectable for the calling thread and requested direction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_selectable ccol_selectable_from_chan(channel *ch, ccol_select_dir dir);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Inspects the calling thread's ID and the requested direction to determine
which of the channel's two underlying circular queues to wrap:

  ccol_select_read; owner <- workers_to_owner_cq  (same as chan_recv_zc)
                    worker <- owner_to_workers_cq
  ccol_select_write; owner -> owner_to_workers_cq  (same as chan_send_zc)
                    worker -> workers_to_owner_cq

The resolution is performed at the call site so that ccol_select() itself
never needs access to the opaque channel struct.  The channel pointer is
not stored; only the resolved circular_queue pointer and the dir value are
kept in the returned ccol_selectable.

The selectable_from_chan() macro is a thin wrapper around this function.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch   Channel to resolve.  Must not be NULL.
dir  ccol_select_read or ccol_select_write.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_selectable of type ccol_selectable_circq wrapping the appropriate
circular_queue for the calling thread and direction.

If ch is NULL, returns a ccol_selectable_circq with a NULL queue pointer.
Passing that to ccol_select() will cause it to return ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Direction is resolved once at call time, not re-evaluated on each
ccol_select() iteration.  If the calling thread's role changes between
construction and the actual ccol_select() call, reconstruct the selectable.

Prefer the selectable_from_chan() macro at call sites for stylistic
consistency with selectable_from_circq() and selectable_from_dynq().
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Reads ch->owner_tid and two pointer fields written once at channel creation
and never modified thereafter; safe to call concurrently from any thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_select (3),
.BR selectable_from_chan (3),
.BR chan_recv_zc (3),
.BR chan_send_zc (3)
